        <item>
        <title>openssl</title>
        <link>https://producthunt.programnotes.cn/en/p/openssl/</link>
        <pubDate>Thu, 07 Aug 2025 15:36:05 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://producthunt.programnotes.cn/en/p/openssl/</guid>
        <description>&lt;img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558032393-d2dd60dae4a0?ixid=M3w0NjAwMjJ8MHwxfHJhbmRvbXx8fHx8fHx8fDE3NTQ1NTIwOTR8&amp;ixlib=rb-4.1.0" alt="Featured image of post openssl" /&gt;&lt;h1 id=&#34;opensslopenssl&#34;&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;openssl/openssl&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h1 id=&#34;welcome-to-the-openssl-project&#34;&gt;Welcome to the OpenSSL Project
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://www.openssl.org&#34;  title=&#34;OpenSSL Homepage&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://producthunt.programnotes.cn/doc/images/openssl.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;openssl logo&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/ci.yml&#34;  title=&#34;GitHub Actions CI&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/workflows/GitHub%20CI/badge.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;github actions ci badge&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/os-zoo.yml&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/os-zoo.yml/badge.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;Nightly OS Zoo ci badge&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/provider-compatibility.yml&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/provider-compatibility.yml/badge.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;Provider Compatibility&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/run_quic_interop.yml&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/run_quic_interop.yml/badge.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;Quic Interop&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/run-checker-daily.yml&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/actions/workflows/run-checker-daily.yml/badge.svg&#34;
	
	
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;Daily checks&#34;
	
	
&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenSSL is a robust, commercial-grade, full-featured Open Source Toolkit
for the TLS (formerly SSL), DTLS and QUIC protocols.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The protocol implementations are based on a full-strength general purpose
cryptographic library, which can also be used stand-alone. Also included is a
cryptographic module validated to conform with FIPS standards.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenSSL is descended from the SSLeay library developed by Eric A. Young
and Tim J. Hudson.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The official Home Page of the OpenSSL Project is &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://www.openssl.org&#34;  title=&#34;OpenSSL Homepage&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;www.openssl.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;table-of-contents&#34;&gt;Table of Contents
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#overview&#34; &gt;Overview&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#download&#34; &gt;Download&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#build-and-install&#34; &gt;Build and Install&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#documentation&#34; &gt;Documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#license&#34; &gt;License&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#support&#34; &gt;Support&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#contributing&#34; &gt;Contributing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#legalities&#34; &gt;Legalities&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;overview&#34;&gt;Overview
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;The OpenSSL toolkit includes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;libssl&lt;/strong&gt;
an implementation of all TLS protocol versions up to TLSv1.3 (&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8446&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;RFC 8446&lt;/a&gt;),
DTLS protocol versions up to DTLSv1.2 (&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6347&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;RFC 6347&lt;/a&gt;) and
the QUIC version 1 protocol (&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9000&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;RFC 9000&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;libcrypto&lt;/strong&gt;
a full-strength general purpose cryptographic library. It constitutes the
basis of the TLS implementation, but can also be used independently.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;openssl&lt;/strong&gt;
the OpenSSL command line tool, a swiss army knife for cryptographic tasks,
testing and analyzing. It can be used for&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;creation of key parameters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;creation of X.509 certificates, CSRs and CRLs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;calculation of message digests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;encryption and decryption&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SSL/TLS/DTLS and client and server tests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;QUIC client tests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;handling of S/MIME signed or encrypted mail&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;and more&amp;hellip;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;download&#34;&gt;Download
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2 id=&#34;for-production-use&#34;&gt;For Production Use
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source code tarballs of the official releases can be downloaded from
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://openssl-library.org/source/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;openssl-library.org/source/&lt;/a&gt;.
The OpenSSL project does not distribute the toolkit in binary form.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, for a large variety of operating systems precompiled versions
of the OpenSSL toolkit are available. In particular, on Linux and other
Unix operating systems, it is normally recommended to link against the
precompiled shared libraries provided by the distributor or vendor.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We also maintain a list of third parties that produce OpenSSL binaries for
various Operating Systems (including Windows) on the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/wiki/Binaries&#34;  title=&#34;List of third party OpenSSL binaries&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Binaries&lt;/a&gt; page on our
wiki.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;for-testing-and-development&#34;&gt;For Testing and Development
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although testing and development could in theory also be done using
the source tarballs, having a local copy of the git repository with
the entire project history gives you much more insight into the
code base.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The main OpenSSL Git repository is private.
There is a public GitHub mirror of it at &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl&#34;  title=&#34;OpenSSL GitHub Mirror&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;github.com/openssl/openssl&lt;/a&gt;,
which is updated automatically from the former on every commit.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A local copy of the Git repository can be obtained by cloning it from
the GitHub mirror using&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone https://github.com/openssl/openssl.git
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you intend to contribute to OpenSSL, either to fix bugs or contribute
new features, you need to fork the GitHub mirror and clone your public fork
instead.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone https://github.com/yourname/openssl.git
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is necessary because all development of OpenSSL nowadays is done via
GitHub pull requests. For more details, see &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;#contributing&#34; &gt;Contributing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;build-and-install&#34;&gt;Build and Install
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;After obtaining the Source, have a look at the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;INSTALL.md&#34; &gt;INSTALL&lt;/a&gt; file for
detailed instructions about building and installing OpenSSL. For some
platforms, the installation instructions are amended by a platform specific
document.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-UNIX.md&#34; &gt;Notes for UNIX-like platforms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-ANDROID.md&#34; &gt;Notes for Android platforms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-WINDOWS.md&#34; &gt;Notes for Windows platforms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-DJGPP.md&#34; &gt;Notes for the DOS platform with DJGPP&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-VMS.md&#34; &gt;Notes for the OpenVMS platform&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-PERL.md&#34; &gt;Notes on Perl&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;NOTES-VALGRIND.md&#34; &gt;Notes on Valgrind&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Specific notes on upgrading to OpenSSL 3.x from previous versions can be found
in the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/master/man7/ossl-guide-migration&#34;  title=&#34;OpenSSL Migration Guide&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;ossl-guide-migration(7ossl)&lt;/a&gt; manual page.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;documentation&#34;&gt;Documentation
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2 id=&#34;readme-files&#34;&gt;README Files
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are some README.md files in the top level of the source distribution
containing additional information on specific topics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;README-QUIC.md&#34; &gt;Information about the OpenSSL QUIC protocol implementation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;README-PROVIDERS.md&#34; &gt;Information about the OpenSSL Provider architecture&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;README-FIPS.md&#34; &gt;Information about using the OpenSSL FIPS validated module&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;README-ENGINES.md&#34; &gt;Information about the legacy OpenSSL Engine architecture&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-openssl-guide&#34;&gt;The OpenSSL Guide
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are some tutorial and introductory pages on some important OpenSSL topics
within the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/master/man7/ossl-guide-introduction&#34;  title=&#34;An introduction to OpenSSL&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;manual-pages&#34;&gt;Manual Pages
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The manual pages for the master branch and all current stable releases are
available online.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/master/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL master&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL 3.5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/3.4/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL 3.4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/3.3/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL 3.3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/3.2/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL 3.2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;OpenSSL 3.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;demos&#34;&gt;Demos
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are numerous source code demos for using various OpenSSL capabilities in the
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;./demos&#34; &gt;demos subfolder&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;wiki&#34;&gt;Wiki
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;There is a &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/openssl/openssl/wiki&#34;  title=&#34;OpenSSL Wiki&#34;
     target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;GitHub Wiki&lt;/a&gt; which is currently not very active.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;license&#34;&gt;License
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;OpenSSL is licensed under the Apache License 2.0, which means that
you are free to get and use it for commercial and non-commercial
purposes as long as you fulfill its conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;See the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;LICENSE.txt&#34; &gt;LICENSE.txt&lt;/a&gt; file for more details.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;support&#34;&gt;Support
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are various ways to get in touch. The correct channel depends on
your requirement. See the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;SUPPORT.md&#34; &gt;SUPPORT&lt;/a&gt; file for more details.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;contributing&#34;&gt;Contributing
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you are interested and willing to contribute to the OpenSSL project,
please take a look at the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;CONTRIBUTING.md&#34; &gt;CONTRIBUTING&lt;/a&gt; file.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;legalities&#34;&gt;Legalities
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;A number of nations restrict the use or export of cryptography. If you are
potentially subject to such restrictions, you should seek legal advice before
attempting to develop or distribute cryptographic code.&lt;/p&gt;
